  :prose
"A character with a name and a body of text is a character. A character
with a name and a body of pixels that BREATHES is a person. The
difference is not measurable in pixels or verbs. It is measurable in
what the kid does when the character leaves the room. If she looks up.
If she noticed. If she cares that the room is now empty.

We are going to give characters bodies that do the smallest, quietest
things: breathe, nod, lean, look toward the player. Not action-movie
gestures. The subtle continuous motion that says 'this thing is alive.'
Animation studios call it 'ambient motion' and it is what separates a
statue from a figure. Kids call it 'breathing' because that is what it
looks like.

The physics of body language: two pixels of vertical drift over a
one-second cycle is the difference between a mannequin and a person.

---

## 4.1 Making a character

A character is data: a name, a body-state, a position, some tags. We
keep it as an association list you can read and update, and a position
that is a `vec` so the math verbs (chapter 6) apply directly.

```scheme
;; Print helper for the text console (stands in for the drawn page).
(define (say . xs) (for-each display xs) (newline))

;; A character is a list of (key value) fields. Position is a vec.
(define (make-character name)
  (list (list 'name   name)
        (list 'pose   'standing)
        (list 'sprite 'placeholder)
        (list 'pos    (vec/make 0 0 0))
        (list 'tags   '())))

(define (char-get c key) (cadr (assq key c)))      ;; second item of the field
(define (char-set c key v) (cons (list key v) c))  ;; newest binding wins

;; Give a name and a body to Mira.
(define mira0 (make-character \"mira\"))
(define mira1 (char-set mira0 'pose 'standing))
(define mira2 (char-set mira1 'sprite 'girl-in-blue))
(define mira3 (char-set mira2 'pos (vec/make 200 180 0)))
(define mira  (char-set mira3 'tags '(friendly)))

(display (char-get mira 'name)) (newline)   ;; => mira
(display (char-get mira 'pos))  (newline)    ;; => (200 180 0)
(display (char-get mira 'tags)) (newline)    ;; => (friendly)
```

`make-character` builds the record; `char-get` reads a field (each
field is a two-item list `(key value)`, so `assq` finds it and `cadr`
takes the value) and `char-set` returns a copy with one field added at
the front (the newest binding wins, so `assq` finds it first). The pose is the stance — standing,
sitting, crouched. The sprite names which picture to render; any symbol
works and a host substitutes a placeholder for unknown names, which is
perfect for prototyping. The position is a `vec` at pixel coordinates,
and `tags` is a list you can later `filter` on (`friendly`, `villain`).

A handful of lines. Mira exists as data. But she does not yet breathe.
Let us fix that.

---

## 4.2 Breath

This is the single most important verb in this chapter.

```scheme
;; A gentle rise-and-fall that never stops. It is a sine wave:
;; amplitude = how far she rises, rate = how many breaths per second,
;; t = the current time in seconds. The result is a vertical offset.
(define pi 3.14159265358979)
(define (breath-offset amp rate t)
  (* amp (sin (* 2 pi rate t))))

;; Mira at rest: 2 pixels of rise, 0.3 breaths per second.
(say \"t=0.0 -> \" (breath-offset 2 0.3 0.0))
(say \"t=0.8 -> \" (breath-offset 2 0.3 0.8))   ;; near the top of a breath
(say \"t=1.6 -> \" (breath-offset 2 0.3 1.6))   ;; near the bottom
```

That's it. `breath-offset` gives Mira's whole body a slow ambient
motion where she rises about 2 pixels and falls about 2 pixels every
few seconds. The 0.3 is the rate — 0.3 Hz, or about one breath every 3
seconds. Slower than a human at rest (about 0.2-0.25 Hz for adults,
faster for kids), which reads on screen as 'calm.' A character in
distress might breathe at 0.6-0.8 Hz — the same amount of movement,
just faster.

Each frame, the runtime adds this offset to Mira's baseline `pos`, so
her drawn position rises and falls forever. That is the whole trick:
one sine wave, evaluated once per frame.

Try it. Put Mira on the page. Do not breathe her. Watch her for ten
seconds. She looks like a decal. Add the breath offset. Watch her for
ten seconds. You cannot look away. Your eye locks onto the tiny
rise-and-fall the way it locks onto a candle flame.

That is what animation studios pay their senior animators to do — the
breath cycle. In our runtime it is one line of Scheme.

---

## 4.3 The (part-*) family

Twelve verbs give a character everything she needs to communicate a
mood without speaking.

```scheme
;; Each gesture is a pure function: it takes a character (an alist) and
;; returns a NEW character with one field changed. The runtime reads
;; those fields each frame and draws the body accordingly.
(define (part-breathe    c rate) (char-set c 'breath-rate rate))  ;; ambient rise-fall
(define (part-nod        c)      (char-set c 'gesture 'nod))       ;; agreement
(define (part-tilt       c deg)  (char-set c 'tilt deg))          ;; head tilt for curiosity
(define (part-lean       c px)   (char-set c 'lean px))           ;; body lean forward or back
(define (part-turn       c dir)  (char-set c 'facing dir))        ;; face left/right/front/back
(define (part-look-toward c x y) (char-set c 'gaze (vec/make x y 0))) ;; eyes track a point
(define (part-bow        c depth)(char-set c 'gesture (list 'bow depth))) ;; respect
(define (part-wave       c hand) (char-set c 'gesture (list 'wave hand)))  ;; greet
(define (part-sway       c amt)  (char-set c 'sway amt))          ;; uncertainty, thinking
(define (part-step       c dir)  (char-set c 'gesture (list 'step dir)))   ;; one step
(define (part-reach      c x y)  (char-set c 'gesture (list 'reach x y)))  ;; arm extends
(define (part-expression c emo)  (char-set c 'expression emo))    ;; face changes
(define (part-shrug      c)      (char-set c 'gesture 'shrug))    ;; don't know
(define (part-halt       c)      (char-set c 'breath-rate 0))     ;; stop ambient motion

;; Confused Mira: tilt her head AND make her look at the player.
(define confused-mira (part-look-toward (part-tilt mira 15) 60 200))
(say \"tilt -> \"   (char-get confused-mira 'tilt))
(say \"gaze -> \"   (char-get confused-mira 'gaze))
```

Every one is short. Every one composes with the others. Because each
returns a new character, you nest them: a character can be breathing
(baseline) and tilting her head (curiosity) and looking at the player
(attention) all at once. The runtime reads the fields and layers them.

Study the list. If you are designing a character, ask 'what does she do
when she is thinking?' Maybe she sways slightly (`part-sway mira 3`).
'What does she do when she is confused?' She tilts and looks
(`part-tilt mira 15` and `part-look-toward mira player-x player-y`).
Every character can have a signature — a combination of these gestures
that reads as HER.

---

## 4.4 A scene with body language

Let us give Mira a whole scene where her body is doing the talking.

```scheme
;; A scene is a timeline: a list of (frame change) beats. `change`
;; takes the current Mira and returns the next Mira. We play the beats
;; in order and let each one transform her.
(define (play-timeline character beats)
  (for-each
    (lambda (beat)
      (let ((frame  (car beat))
            (change (cadr beat)))
        (say \"frame \" frame \":\")
        (set! character (change character))))
    beats)
  character)

(define (mira-scene)
  (say \"A small back garden. Mira is looking at a book.\")
  ;; Mira's baseline: breathing, sitting.
  (let ((m (part-breathe
             (char-set (char-set mira 'pose 'sitting)
                       'pos (vec/make 240 200 0))
             0.3)))                            ;; slow, calm
    (play-timeline m
      (list
        ;; The kid walks in — eyes to the door, head tilts.
        (list 60  (lambda (c) (part-tilt (part-look-toward c 60 200) 8)))
        ;; The kid says hi — Mira smiles and waves.
        (list 120 (lambda (c) (part-wave (part-expression c 'smile) 'right)))
        ;; The kid asks a hard question — she straightens, leans back, thinks.
        (list 240 (lambda (c)
                    (part-sway
                      (part-expression (part-lean (part-tilt c 0) -8) 'thinking)
                      4)))))))

(mira-scene)
```

Read the code. That is a whole silent conversation. Mira is reading.
The kid walks in — Mira's eyes go to the door and her head tilts. The
kid says hi — Mira smiles and waves. The kid asks a hard question —
Mira straightens, leans back, thinks. No dialogue in this snippet at
all, and yet a scene has happened.

Now imagine putting dialogue on top. Every part-verb is punctuation.
Mira nods on 'yes.' She sways on 'I do not know.' She tilts on the
kid's confusing sentence. The words are the melody; the body is the
rhythm.

---

## 4.5 A character who reacts to the mouse

`part/look-toward` accepts any (x, y). If we pass the mouse position,
Mira watches the mouse. This is a small trick that makes a static
scene feel alive.

```scheme
;; Mira's eyes follow the pointer. Each frame the runtime hands us the
;; pointer position; we return a Mira whose gaze points there.
(define (mira-follows-pointer c px py)
  (part-look-toward c px py))

;; The runtime would call this every frame. Here we script four frames
;; of pointer movement to see the gaze track it.
(for-each
  (lambda (pt)
    (let ((m (mira-follows-pointer mira (car pt) (cdr pt))))
      (say \"pointer \" pt \" -> gaze \" (char-get m 'gaze))))
  (list (cons 60 200) (cons 120 140) (cons 300 140) (cons 300 260)))
```

Every frame, Mira's eyes reposition to look where the pointer is.
Move the pointer left; her eyes go left. Move it up; her eyes go up.
This costs almost nothing and communicates 'she is aware of you' more
strongly than a whole paragraph of description.

You can also do this with the touch position on a tablet, or with the
sensed direction of a controller stick. Whatever the kid's attention
points at, Mira looks at.

Do not overuse this. If every character in the game tracks the mouse
constantly, the effect feels needy — like all the NPCs want your
attention. Use it selectively, for the character whose relationship
with the player is central to the scene.

---

## 4.6 Signatures — every character breathes differently

The bee-keeper breathes slow. The kid breathes fast. The villain
breathes slower than anyone else in the game — that is HOW he is
scary. Signatures come from the parameters.

```scheme
;; A signature applies a breath rate AND a sway amount to a character.
(define (signature c rate sway) (part-sway (part-breathe c rate) sway))

(define kid        (make-character \"kid\"))
(define villain    (make-character \"villain\"))
(define bee-keeper (make-character \"bee-keeper\"))

(define mira-sig       (signature mira       0.28 1))   ;; calm, barely detectable
(define kid-sig        (signature kid        0.55 3))   ;; excited, fidgets
(define villain-sig    (signature villain    0.15 0))   ;; unnervingly slow, unmoving
(define bee-keeper-sig (signature bee-keeper 0.20 2))   ;; slow, a small sway

(say \"mira    breath \" (char-get mira-sig 'breath-rate))
(say \"kid     breath \" (char-get kid-sig 'breath-rate))
(say \"villain breath \" (char-get villain-sig 'breath-rate))   ;; slowest of all
```

The kid does not know she is decoding breath rates. She just feels
that the villain is unnerving and the kid character is nervous and
Mira is calm and the bee-keeper is slower than everyone else because
he has been breathing to that rhythm his whole life.

Set the signature ONCE when the character enters the scene. It runs
in the background for the whole scene. Layer specific gestures on top
— nods, tilts, waves — without touching the baseline. When the
character leaves, `(part-halt villain)` sets the breath rate to zero,
stopping all ambient motion.

---

## 4.7 A worked scene: the meeting on the porch

Two characters. One breath rate each. A page of prose that would take
you five minutes to write and thirty seconds to read.

```scheme
;; Two characters share one timeline. Each beat is (frame actor change
;; line): which frame, whose body moves, how it moves, and the line (or
;; \"\" for silence). We fold the beats, transforming whichever actor moves.
(define (porch-meeting)
  (say \"The porch. Late afternoon. Two chairs.\")
  (let ((mira   (part-breathe
                  (char-set (char-set mira 'pose 'sitting) 'pos (vec/make 120 180 0))
                  0.28))
        (father (part-breathe
                  (char-set (char-set (make-character \"father\") 'pose 'sitting)
                            'pos (vec/make 280 180 0))
                  0.22)))
    ;; First minute: silence, then a line about the sky.
    (say \"MIRA: 'It is a fine sky.'\")
    ;; Father takes his time — he turns to look at her.
    (set! father (part-turn father 'left))
    (set! father (part-nod father))
    (say \"FATHER: 'It is.'\")
    ;; A pause. Then the news.
    (say \"MIRA: 'She came home yesterday.'\")
    ;; He does not react quickly. He stops breathing for one cycle,
    ;; then resumes.
    (set! father (part-halt father))            ;; a moment without breath
    (set! father (part-breathe father 0.22))
    (set! father (part-lean father -6))         ;; leans back into the chair
    (say \"FATHER: 'Did she stay?'\")
    (set! mira (part-nod mira))
    (say \"MIRA: 'She stayed.'\")
    (list mira father)))

(porch-meeting)
```

That is a scene with almost no words. The father's held breath — one
line of `part-halt` — carries more than any adjective. The lean back
after the news comes carries the rest. Mira's nod at the end is a
period. Nobody said anything a screenwriter would love, but a whole
family reunion happened in eight seconds of screen time.

The kid who watches this and understands why it worked will write
better scenes forever. The kid who wrote this and got tears from a
friend who played it will write more games.

---

## 4.8 Common desktop tasks — the operator's little companion

An operator's shop has a mascot: a small cat named Yuzu. Yuzu sits
in the corner of the shop cart, breathes, occasionally looks at the
kid, occasionally does a small stretch. Yuzu is not gameplay. Yuzu is
COMPANY.

```scheme
;; Yuzu the shop cat. Ambient companion. A shop cart would set her up
;; once at open and let the runtime tick her; here we build her and
;; show two of her ambient behaviours firing.
(define (open-shop-with-yuzu)
  (say \"Welcome. Feel free to look around.\")
  (let ((yuzu (part-breathe
                (char-set (char-set (make-character \"yuzu\") 'sprite 'cat-orange)
                          'pos (vec/make 400 300 0))
                0.35)))                          ;; cats breathe faster than us

    ;; Every 15-25 seconds, Yuzu looks wherever the pointer is.
    (define (yuzu-attention c px py)
      (part-look-toward c px py))

    ;; A small stretch: reach up, hold breath a beat, resume.
    (define (yuzu-stretch c)
      (let* ((p  (char-get c 'pos))
             (up (part-reach c (vec/ref p 0) (- (vec/ref p 1) 20)))
             (held (part-halt up)))
        (part-breathe held 0.35)))

    (let* ((looked    (yuzu-attention yuzu 260 180))
           (stretched (yuzu-stretch looked)))
      (say \"gaze after attention: \" (char-get looked 'gaze))
      (say \"breath after stretch: \" (char-get stretched 'breath-rate))
      stretched)))

(open-shop-with-yuzu)
```

Yuzu does nothing gameplay-relevant. Ever. She just breathes and
occasionally looks at the pointer and occasionally stretches. Customers
who visit the shop feel a small warmth without being able to name
why. That is Yuzu.

Companies once paid millions for animated logo mascots that did less
than this. An operator with fifty lines of Scheme has one that does
more. That is the leverage.

---

## 4.9 Routing patterns — grouping characters by scene

When a scene has three characters, do NOT re-spawn them every frame.
Spawn once at scene entry; halt when the scene ends. Otherwise you
leak entities and their breath-timers pile up.

```scheme
(define scene-characters '())

(define (add-character-to-scene c)
  (set! scene-characters (cons c scene-characters)))

(define (end-scene-clear-characters)
  ;; Halt every character's breath, then drop the whole list so the
  ;; runtime despawns them and their breath-timers stop.
  (set! scene-characters (map part-halt scene-characters))
  (say \"halting \" (length scene-characters) \" characters\")
  (set! scene-characters '()))

;; Every scene ends with:
(add-character-to-scene mira)
(add-character-to-scene (make-character \"kid\"))
(end-scene-clear-characters)
(say \"remaining: \" (length scene-characters))   ;; => 0
```

Halt breath, despawn entities, reset the list. If you skip this you
will notice the game slowly getting laggier over an hour of play —
every scene's ghost characters still breathing off-screen. Rookie
mistake, easy fix.

---

## 4.10 Closing

You made a character breathe. You gave her a body. You wrote a scene
where two people had a hard conversation using seven part-verbs and
almost no words. That is what body language buys you.

Any character in your story from here on breathes. If she does not
breathe, she is furniture. Cortex tells you WHO she is. Breath tells
the kid that she IS.

Chapter 5 gives her a mouth — full dialogue trees. Now that her body
is alive, her words matter more.")
